# Target C — near-Mantel endpoint proof repair

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** proof-only repair note for `target_c_near_mantel_atomic_endpoint_exclusion.md`; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This note expands two compressed steps in the near-Mantel endpoint exclusion. It does
not change its statement or constants.

## 1. Atomic equality extraction is an equality case of the §142 measure sandwich

Use exactly the normalization of audited-working §142. Let `mu_D` be the empirical
projective train-phase measure and let `nu_D` be the normalized crossing measure used
there. Along the endpoint sequence,

\[
{T\over D}\to x,
\qquad
{R\over T^2}\to{\Delta_2\over x^2},
\]

and after a subsequence

\[
\mu_D\Rightarrow\mu,
\qquad
\nu_D\Rightarrow\nu.
\]

Write `rho` for the total atomic mass of `mu`. The proof of §142 gives the chain

\[
{\Delta_2\over x^2}
=\|\nu\|
\le \mathcal M(\mu)
\le {\rho^2\over4},
\tag{R.1}
\]

where `mathcal M(mu)` is the product-measure capacity of the exact projective
`pi/3` relation, with the same unoriented-edge normalization as §142. The second
inequality is the weighted Mantel bound on the atomic relation graph. No new
factor-of-two convention is introduced here.

The endpoint proof already derives

\[
x\rho=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\tag{R.2}
\]

Therefore the two ends of (R.1) are equal:

\[
{\Delta_2\over x^2}={\rho^2\over4}.
\]

Hence every inequality in (R.1) is equality. In particular:

1. the limiting crossing measure loses no mass outside the exact `pi/3` relation;
2. it loses no mass to the nonatomic part of `mu`; and
3. the atomic relation graph saturates its weighted Mantel capacity.

To pass back to finite trains, enumerate the atoms of `mu`. For each `j`, choose
disjoint phase neighborhoods around the first `j` atoms whose boundaries have
`mu`-measure zero. First let `D->infinity` at fixed `j`, then let `j->infinity`.
Equality in (R.1) implies that the train count captured by these neighborhoods tends
to `rho T` and the number of crossings with both ends captured tends to all of `R`.
A diagonal choice therefore gives a finite induced train graph `G_D` with

\[
|V(G_D)|=(\rho x+o(1))D
=(2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+o(1))D,
\]

\[
|E(G_D)|=(\Delta_2-o(1))D^2
={|V(G_D)|^2\over4}-o(D^2).
\tag{R.3}
\]

This is the precise justification for equation (NM.16). It does not assert that the
limiting atomic support is finite.

## 2. The ordering coordinate and the diameter projection are different

Lemma NM.2 uses two linear directions for two different purposes. Conflating them
would create a real orientation error.

Let the two contact-direction families have lifted arguments

\[
\alpha=\alpha_0+O(\rho_D),
\qquad
\beta=\alpha_0+{\pi\over3}+O(\rho_D),
\qquad \rho_D\to0.
\]

### 2.1 Order all trains using their canonical orientations

For a train with contact direction `alpha`, canonical midpoint displacement lies in

\[
(\alpha+\pi/3,\alpha+2\pi/3).
\]

For the second family, in its **canonical** orientation, midpoint displacement lies in

\[
(\beta+\pi/3,\beta+2\pi/3).
\]

The union of these two cone families has angular span

\[
{2\pi\over3}+O(\rho_D)<\pi
\]

for large `D`. Thus one order coordinate `X` is strictly increasing on every selected
train in canonical orientation. This is the common-half-plane step used in
proof-only §145; it does not require every possible cross-family pair to cross and it
does not require a strict phase threshold between all train potentials.

For every **actual** cross-family crossing, strict-rhombus geometry forces the lifted
phase difference to be `pi/3+positive`, because a difference below `pi/3` cannot be a
strict-rhombus crossing. Hence all actual crossings have one common signed graph
crossing in the `X` coordinate.

After the high-degree pruning in NM.2, every two trains on one shore have a common
neighbor and every three have a common neighbor on the opposite shore. Applying the
pair-order argument of canonical Lemma 76.1 to a common neighbor makes the relative
order of each pair independent of which common neighbor is used. Applying it to a
common neighbor of each triple makes those pairwise relations transitive. Therefore
each shore has a genuine global total order, and every train sees its actual neighbors
in the restriction of the opposite global order.

### 2.2 Build the diameter path by reversing the second train

Let `B+` be maximal in the global `B` order and let `A+` be maximal among the actual
neighbors of `B+` in the global `A` order. Then `B+` is the last actual retained
`B`-neighbor encountered along `A+` in canonical orientation, and `A+` is the last
actual retained `A`-neighbor encountered along `B+` in canonical orientation.

The diameter path therefore follows

1. `A+` in canonical orientation, from its first retained `B` crossing to `B+`; then
2. `B+` in the **reverse** of its canonical orientation, from `A+` to its first
   retained `A` crossing.

This reversal is deliberate. The order was established before the reversal and is
unchanged as an unoriented order; reversing the second leg makes the selected crossing
vectors point in the useful directions.

Let `p` have argument

\[
\alpha_0+{\pi\over6}.
\]

At an `A`--`B` crossing, the selected displacement on the canonical `A` leg is the
unit `B` contact vector, with argument `beta`, while on the reversed `B` leg it is the
unit `A` contact vector, with argument `alpha`. Hence each selected vector has
projection

\[
p\cdot u,\ p\cdot v
\ge {\sqrt3\over2}-O(\rho_D).
\tag{R.4}
\]

For an arbitrary intervening rhombus on the canonical `A` leg, its midpoint
displacement has argument in

\[
(\alpha+\pi/3,\alpha+2\pi/3),
\]

so its `p`-projection is at least `-O(rho_D)`. For an arbitrary intervening rhombus on
the **reversed** `B` leg, its midpoint displacement has argument in

\[
(\beta-2\pi/3,\beta-\pi/3),
\]

and again its `p`-projection is at least `-O(rho_D)`.

Finally, a complete stopped train crosses at most

\[
{2D\over\sqrt3}
\tag{R.5}
\]

strict rhombi. Indeed its canonical transverse coordinate increases by exactly
`sin(theta)>=sqrt(3)/2` in every crossed strict rhombus, while its two terminal contact
edge midpoints lie in `conv(A)` and hence have transverse separation at most the source
diameter `D`. Thus all intervening-rhombus losses on the two path legs total only
`O(rho_D D)=o(D)`.

The path endpoints are centers of actual source rhombi, hence are convex combinations
of source points and have mutual distance at most `D`. The usual endpoint half-vector
loss is `O(1)`. Therefore

\[
D\ge
\left({\sqrt3\over2}-o(1)\right)
\bigl(\deg_B(A^+)+\deg_A(B^+)\bigr),
\]

and the high-degree pruning makes the two degrees equal to the corresponding shore
sizes up to `o(D)`. This is exactly (NM.23).

## 3. Repair disposition

The two potentially ambiguous steps are therefore resolved without adding hypotheses:

- the atomic finite graph follows from equality throughout the exact §142
  crossing-measure/product-measure/weighted-Mantel sandwich;
- train ordering uses canonical orientations and a dedicated order coordinate, while
  the diameter estimate separately reverses the second path leg and uses the
  `pi/6` midpoint projection.

No complete biclique, disk topology, source deletion, or full-source affine-kernel
extension is used.
